---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Site
  title="About the project"
  description="KajLights helps people learn electronics, computing and networking by making things with microcontrollers. Understanding how things work gives you more ideas for what to build."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">WHY KAJLIGHTS EXISTS</p>
    <h1>
      Understand more.
      <br />
      Imagine more.
    </h1>
    <p class="intro">
      KajLights helps people learn electronics, computing and networking by
      making things with microcontrollers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Start with something you can see: a light changes colour, a pattern moves,
      or a message switches an animation. Follow what made it happen. There is a
      circuit supplying power, a computer running instructions and a signal
      carrying information. Learning how these parts work together gives you
      ideas for projects of your own.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose">
    <h2>One project, several things to discover</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose you want a light outside your door to show whether you are busy.
      First, connect the lights and choose a colour. Then make a pattern that
      someone else can recognise. Later, explore how to change it from another
      device. Along the way you meet electrical connections, instructions,
      memory and messages, each with a job in something you are making.
    </p>
    <p>
      Coding is one part of this. Recognising a component, tracing a wire,
      working out where a program runs and finding why a message did not arrive
      are part of understanding a computer system too.
    </p>
    <h2 id="why-understanding">Understanding gives you more to ask for</h2>
    <blockquote class="learning-prompt">
      If you can’t imagine it, you can’t ask for it to be built.
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      AI can help turn an idea into a program or a design. Experience with the
      parts gives you more ideas to bring to it. Once you know a board can keep
      time, control individual lights and receive messages, you can imagine a
      countdown, a game or a display that responds to another device.
    </p>
    <p>
      That understanding also makes a request clearer. “Make something with
      lights” can become “Make a countdown that turns one light off each second,
      then flashes when time is up.” You can say what should happen, try it, and
      check whether the result matches your idea. These skills help whether you
      build by yourself, with other people or with AI.
    </p>
    <h2>Make something, then follow your curiosity</h2>
    <p>
      The first build is intended to be achievable for a child of about ten with
      an adult helping prepare the board and check the wiring. Get a light
      working, change it, and find out why it changed. More detailed
      explanations are there when a question or the next project calls for them.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also start with the browser lessons, investigate an unexpected
      result or adapt an example. The{" "}
      <a href={`${base}learn/`}>learning page</a> offers starting points in
      electronics, computing and communications. The twelve programming lessons
      are available now; the hardware and networking activities are an early
      collection we want to develop further.
    </p>
    <h2>A shared aim with Found in Space</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://foundin.space/">Found in Space</a> lets people explore
      scientific data and models, understand what they show, and use them to
      make something new. KajLights takes the same approach to circuits and
      computers: give people something to explore, a way to understand it and
      materials they can build with.
    </p>
    <h2>The projects behind the lights</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm">LightVM</a> runs lighting
      programs on microcontrollers and in a browser preview. Its editor offers
      Python, JavaScript and visual blocks. This website provides the build
      guide, learning activities and tools for connecting and setting up
      devices.
    </p>
    <p>
      The companion <strong>kaj-fi</strong> project analyses sound and sends
      measurements, such as sound level, to other devices. This makes it
      possible to explore lights that respond to sound. kaj-fi is still in
      development; its public firmware downloads are not available yet.
    </p>
    <h2>Early testing</h2>
    <p>
      KajLights is an experimental project. Expect bugs, unfinished features and
      instructions that need improving. Some changes require setting up a board
      again. Check the <a href={`${base}releases/`}>firmware catalogue</a>{" "}
      before buying hardware: a board we develop for may not yet have an
      installer ready to use.
    </p>
    <p>
      The activities have not yet been evaluated in classrooms. Feedback from
      learners, parents and teachers will help us improve both the explanations
      and the projects.
    </p>
    <h2>Made by Kaj, open to others</h2>
    <p>
      KajLights is built by Kaj Siebert. The source code and examples are
      available through <a href="https://github.com/KajLights">GitHub</a>. You
      can use the browser tools without a KajLights account. Connections to your
      board go directly from your browser; the list of remembered devices stays
      in that browser.
    </p>
    <p>
      A clearer explanation, a useful picture or an experiment is a welcome
      contribution.{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/KajLights.github.io/issues">
        Tell us what you tried and what could be better
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </section>
</Site>
